Title: LuaSocket and ENet over bsdsocket.library
Repository: love
Base: def245102c67909f9902095af613b1ae05249b62
Requires: preceding patches in series
Date: 2026-11-20
Author: aros-loeve project
Problem: The AROS build left out LuaSocket and ENet (patch 0003): require "socket" and require "enet" failed, so games with network play, HTTP requests or a debugger connection could not start. Both libraries expect sockets to be C library descriptors (close, select, fcntl, errno); on AROS sockets belong to bsdsocket.library and to the task that opened it.
Change: src/libraries/aros gives each thread its own bsdsocket.library base (opened on first use, closed when the thread ends) with its own errno and h_errno, and maps the BSD names to calls through it; the inet_* names that AROS's <arpa/inet.h> renames to missing C library functions go to the library too. LuaSocket's Unix layer and ENet's use it for close, select, non-blocking mode and name lookup (getaddrinfo, getnameinfo of the Roadshow part, built with __CONFIG_ROADSHOW__). Without a running network stack require "socket" and enet's start fail with an error. Unix domain sockets and serial descriptors are left out. lua-https stays out.
Scope: AROS (all targets)
Upstream: local-only
Verification: mainline x86_64 (nightly 2026-11-15, AROSTCP, QEMU user network), 2026-11-20, one test game: LuaSocket 3.1.0 loads; socket.dns.toip; TCP to a public web server (HTTP/1.1 200 OK) and socket.http (200, 577 bytes); UDP and TCP between two sockets on 127.0.0.1 with socket.select; a refused connection reports "connection refused"; an ENet client and server on 127.0.0.1 connect and pass a packet; a LOVE thread opens its own connection, and the main thread's sockets work after that thread ended; the program exits normally. Not run: Raspberry Pi, a system without a network stack, a socket handed from one thread to another (not supported), long transfers.
Removal: not planned
---
